# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ImmigrationClock environment variables
# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in real values.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public site URL — used for canonical URLs, sitemap.xml, robots, and OpenGraph
# tags. Defaults to https://immigrationclock.com when unset, so production is
# correct without config. Set this for local dev or preview deploys if needed.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLIC IDENTITY — claims the site makes about itself.
#
# Both default to empty and stay hidden until set. Do NOT fill these in with a
# plausible-looking guess: `sameAs` in structured data tells search engines we
# own that account, and a contact address that nobody reads is worse than no
# address at all. Set them once the account and inbox genuinely exist.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_HANDLE=""
#
# REQUIRED FOR A NEWSLETTER SEND. Unset, the broadcast carries no Reply-To and
# the archived edition silently drops its "Contact" footer link — a quietly
# different newsletter that nobody can reply to. Newsletter preflight now treats
# it as a blocking error and a live send refuses rather than omitting it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REVENUE: affiliate / partner links (see src/lib/partners.ts and /resources).
# The "Helpful services" modules and the /resources page link to services
# newcomers use (immigration legal help, money transfer, nonresident tax,
# newcomer banking, insurance, eSIM). Out of the box they point to each
# service's homepage. To EARN, map partner ids -> your tracked affiliate/
# referral URLs as a JSON object (no code changes needed). Any id you omit
# keeps its homepage default. Partner ids: boundless, attorney-match, wise,
# remitly, sprintax, resident-tax, newcomer-credit, newcomer-insurance, esim,
# citizenship-prep, credential-evaluation, document-translation, visa-jobs,
# credit-builder, intl-moving, english-prep.
#   Example:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PARTNER_LINKS={"wise":"https://wise.com/invite/abc","sprintax":"https://www.sprintax.com/?ref=you"}
NEXT_PUBLIC_PARTNER_LINKS=""

# Optional "tip jar" — a Buy Me a Coffee / Ko-fi / GitHub Sponsors URL. When set,
# a "♥ Support this project" link appears in the footer. Hidden until configured.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_URL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANALYTICS (optional but strongly recommended — this is how you SEE what earns).
# Once either is set, the partner-click and key-date events the site already
# fires start recording, with the ?subid=ic-<placement> attribution so you can
# tell which module/page drives revenue. Both are off until set.
#
# Plausible — privacy-first, cookieless, no consent banner needed. Set to your
# verified domain (e.g. immigrationclock.vercel.app). Recommended.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN=""
# Google Analytics 4 measurement id (e.g. G-XXXXXXXXXX). Uses cookies, so it only
# loads after the visitor accepts the cookie banner.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEWSLETTER — "Immigration Pulse" (Buttondown)
#
# Set your Buttondown newsletter username (the one in your Buttondown URL, e.g.
# "immigrationclock" for buttondown.com/immigrationclock). The signup form posts
# to Buttondown's public embed endpoint — no API key is involved and nothing
# secret is exposed to the browser.
#
# Until this is set, the signup renders an honest "not open for signups yet"
# message and does NOT show an email field. It will never claim someone was
# subscribed when no provider received their address.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BUTTONDOWN_USERNAME=""

# Optional: full subscribe URL for a different provider (Mailchimp, ConvertKit,
# Formspree…). Takes precedence over NEXT_PUBLIC_BUTTONDOWN_USERNAME. The form
# POSTs a field named `email`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NEWSLETTER_ENDPOINT=""

# Set to "dev" on local and preview deployments to disable submission entirely,
# so test addresses can never reach the live audience. Leave empty in production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NEWSLETTER_MODE=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESEND — the default newsletter provider, and the ONLY server-side secrets
# this project has.
#
# This single key switches on /api/subscribe and makes the signup form appear.
# Without it the form stays hidden and the route refuses, because asking for an
# address we cannot store is the failure this codebase has already made once
# (docs/data-corrections.md).
#
# The key must have FULL ACCESS. A sending-only key can deliver the welcome
# email but cannot create contacts, which produces the confusing symptom of
# subscribers receiving mail while the contact list stays empty. Verify with
# `node scripts/verify-resend.mjs`.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY has NO NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and must never get one. A
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is inlined into the JavaScript bundle — prefixing this
# key would publish it to every visitor.
#
# Read at BUILD time to decide whether to render the form, so adding these to
# Vercel requires a redeploy before signups turn on.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
# Sender for transactional newsletter mail. Must be on a domain verified in
# Resend. Defaults to "Immigration Clock <noreply@immigrationclock.com>".
# Keep this separate from the human contact inbox: replies go to
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL via the Reply-To header.
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=""

# The Resend SEGMENT every new subscriber is added to — "Immigration Pulse".
#
# A contact in Resend is account-level and belongs to no list by itself. The
# segment is what a Broadcast targets, so a contact created WITHOUT this is
# stored and unreachable: the signup works, the dashboard fills up, and nobody
# ever receives an issue. /api/subscribe logs loudly when this is unset.
#
# This is an identifier, not a credential — it is safe in a deployment's
# environment settings and is deliberately NOT hard-coded in source, so staging
# and production can point at different segments. It still does not belong in
# git, which is why this file carries an empty placeholder.
#
# Find it in the Resend dashboard under Audiences → Segments; the id is the UUID
# in the URL. Applied on both signup paths, so re-submitting an address that is
# already a contact repairs its membership instead of doing nothing.
RESEND_NEWSLETTER_SEGMENT_ID=""

# TEST SEAM — leave empty everywhere real. Points the subscribe route at a stub
# so the signup flow can be exercised end-to-end without a live key or a real
# audience. Setting this in production would send subscribers nowhere.
RESEND_API_BASE=""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATA SOURCES — keys for government APIs that require one.
#
# Every other source ImmigrationClock ingests is keyless: the Federal Register,
# USCIS RSS, the USCIS Policy Manual, and CourtListener all work without
# credentials. Congress is the exception.
#
# CONGRESS_API_KEY — free, from https://api.congress.gov. Without it the
# Congress adapter reports itself as UNCONFIGURED and returns no events; it does
# not fail the build, because an unconfigured source is a known gap rather than
# an outage, and conflating the two would hide a real outage.
#
# Do NOT use the literal "DEMO_KEY": it works, but is rate-limited to roughly
# 50 requests a day, so it would appear to succeed in testing and silently
# truncate in production. The adapter treats DEMO_KEY as unset for that reason.
CONGRESS_API_KEY=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD-TIME OPERATIONAL KNOBS
#
# None of these belong in a hosting provider's environment settings — they are
# switches for running the data pipeline by hand, and the defaults are correct
# for production. Listed here so an operator can discover them without reading
# the scripts.
#
# EVENTS_SINCE    How far back the event build looks. Default: 90 days.
#                 Set to backfill: EVENTS_SINCE=2025-01-01 npm run build:events
# EVENTS_LIMIT    Per-adapter cap for one run. Default 250. Raise for a deep
#                 backfill; the build reports when the cap actually truncates.
# EVENTS_OFFLINE  Set to 1 to validate the committed store with no network.
#                 Used by CI checks that must not hit government APIs.
# ALLOW_STALE_EMPLOYERS
#                 Lets the employer build proceed on a stale snapshot instead of
#                 failing. For local work only — production should fail loudly
#                 rather than publish figures it could not refresh.
# WARN_SCRAPE_DIR Where the WARN scraper writes its snapshot. Defaults to the
#                 repository path used by the scheduled workflow.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SOCIAL PUBLISHING — X and LinkedIn, three slots a day (see docs/social.md)
#
# Nothing here is required to build or run the site. The whole subsystem is
# inert until SOCIAL_POST_ENABLED is set to exactly "true", and in CI every
# value below comes from GitHub Secrets rather than a file.
#
# THE MASTER SWITCH. Unset or anything other than "true" means every run
# selects, generates and validates as normal and then publishes nothing. This
# is the shipped state: the platform adapters and the Anthropic call shape have
# not yet been verified against a live endpoint. See docs/social.md §Going live.
SOCIAL_POST_ENABLED=""

# The copy engine. Required for a live run; without it every slot skips with
# SKIPPED_ENGINE_UNAVAILABLE rather than publishing something a model did not
# write. There is deliberately no template fallback.
OPENAI_API_KEY=""
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# Which provider to use: "openai" (default), "anthropic", or "transcript".
SOCIAL_ENGINE=""

# Optional model override. Defaults to claude-opus-5. The seam exists so a model
# change is configuration rather than an architecture change.
SOCIAL_MODEL=""

# X — OAuth 1.0a user context. All four are needed; three of four means X skips.
# OAuth 1.0a rather than 2.0 for one reason: these tokens do NOT expire, which
# is what lets X genuinely run unattended.
X_API_KEY=""
X_API_SECRET=""
X_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
X_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# LinkedIn. The access token EXPIRES roughly every 60 days and programmatic
# refresh is gated behind an approved-partner product, so this is the one place
# the system is not truly unattended. When the token lapses LinkedIn skips with
# SKIPPED_CREDENTIAL_EXPIRED and X carries on untouched — renew the token and
# LinkedIn resumes on the next slot.
#
# LINKEDIN_AUTHOR_URN is the posting identity, e.g. urn:li:organization:12345
LINKEDIN_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
LINKEDIN_AUTHOR_URN=""

# Where the post ledger lives. Defaults to src/lib/generated/social-posted.json.
# Set this in tests so a run cannot write to the real ledger.
SOCIAL_POST_LEDGER=""
